🔴 Vulnerability Title

Misconfigured Content Security Policy (CSP) Allowing Unsafe Inline and Eval Execution

📊 Risk Level

🔴 High

📉 CVSS 3.1 Score

7.4 (High)
Vector: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:C/C:L/I:L/A:N

🏷️ Vulnerability Category
Security Misconfiguration
Client-Side Injection (XSS Protection Weakness)
📖 Description

It was observed that the application implements a Content Security Policy (CSP) that is overly permissive and includes insecure directives such as 'unsafe-inline', 'unsafe-eval', and wildcard sources (*).

From the response headers:

Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self' * data: https: blob:;
script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval';

The CSP configuration allows:

Execution of inline JavaScript (unsafe-inline)
Execution of dynamically evaluated code (unsafe-eval)
Loading resources from any domain (*)

This effectively weakens the protection against Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) and allows attackers to execute arbitrary scripts if any injection point exists.

💥 Impact
Enables Reflected/Stored/DOM-based XSS attacks
Allows execution of malicious inline scripts
Increases risk of session hijacking
Data exfiltration (cookies, tokens, sensitive information)
Bypasses intended browser security controls
🛠️ Recommendations

It is recommended to implement the following security measures:

Remove unsafe directives:
'unsafe-inline'
'unsafe-eval'
Avoid wildcard sources:
Replace * with strict domain allowlist
Implement strict CSP:
Content-Security-Policy:
default-src 'self';
script-src 'self' https://trusted-cdn.com;
object-src 'none';
base-uri 'self';
frame-ancestors 'self';
Use nonce-based or hash-based CSP:
script-src 'self' 'nonce-<random>';
Regularly audit CSP policies:
Use tools like CSP Evaluator
